<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!-- Owner direction, verbatim: "Add configurable company roles, staff schedules,
       certifications, training jobs, field history, trust/stress/exposure and equipment
       familiarity; preserve pawn autonomy and vanilla skill/trait systems".

       Roles, field history, exposure and equipment familiarity all shipped earlier.
       CERTIFICATIONS AND TRAINING JOBS were the remainder on that row.

       A certification is the PERSON-LEVEL twin of a company project. A project spends insight
       and work to give the whole branch a capability; a certification spends one person's work
       to give that person a qualification, and it stays with them rather than with the branch.
       The owner's row lists roles and certifications separately, and that is the difference: a
       role is what somebody is assigned to do, a certification is what they were trained to do.

       THE TRAINING JOB IS A REAL BILL. Each one names a RecipeDef the player adds to a bench,
       exactly like RR_AssembleMachineGate: a pawn walks over, does the work, and
       RecipeWorker_RimroomsCertification records it against whoever did it. No new work giver,
       no new job driver and no new building - Core's bill system already IS this mechanism.

       AND CORE ENFORCES THE SKILL FLOOR, not this mod: the recipes carry skillRequirements, so
       RimWorld itself refuses the bill to an unqualified pawn. A floor checked after the work
       was finished would mean spending six thousand ticks of somebody's day and being told no
       at the end.

       EVERY ONE OF THESE IS READ BY SOMETHING. That rule is RimroomsProjectDef's and it is the
       one worth keeping: "an unlock a player is told about and that changes nothing is worse
       than no unlock: it is a lie on the card." proof-certifications.py asserts it.

         RR_Cert_GateOperator       -> GateSpinUp, a trained operator dials faster
         RR_Cert_FieldAnalyst       -> EvidenceReview, qualifies to sign off a report
         RR_Cert_ReserveTechnician  -> JobDriver_RimroomsGate, reconditioning is less work

       SECURITY AND MEDICAL LOGISTICS HAVE NO CERTIFICATION, DELIBERATELY. There is no existing
       surface that would act on one, and a fourth and fifth card promising nothing is exactly
       what the project tree refuses by leaving transport and orbital support without a tier 0.
       Stated here rather than padded out. -->

  <RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>
    <defName>RR_Cert_GateOperator</defName>
    <label>gate operator</label>
    <description>Trained on the dialling procedure: what the reserve is doing, which readings mean stop, and how to hold a ramp without watching it climb.

A trained operator takes work off the spin-up. It stacks with having personally walked the address, because being taught the procedure and having been down that route are two different things.</description>
    <role>operations</role>
    <recipeDefName>RR_TrainGateOperator</recipeDefName>
  </RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>

  <RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>
    <defName>RR_Cert_FieldAnalyst</defName>
    <label>field analyst</label>
    <description>Trained to read somebody else's report and say whether the branch can stand behind it.

Qualifies to sign off a finished analysis whatever their Intellectual. It only ever widens who can review - everybody who could before still can.</description>
    <role>research</role>
    <recipeDefName>RR_TrainFieldAnalyst</recipeDefName>
  </RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>

  <RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>
    <defName>RR_Cert_ReserveTechnician</defName>
    <label>reserve technician</label>
    <description>Trained on the gate's own wear: which parts go first, and what reconditioning actually has to touch.

Reconditioning a gate takes a trained technician noticeably less work. An untrained branch services the gate on exactly the terms it always did.</description>
    <role>engineering</role>
    <recipeDefName>RR_TrainReserveTechnician</recipeDefName>
  </RimroomsAsyncIndustries.Personnel.RimroomsCertificationDef>

</Defs>
